```{r setup, include = FALSE}
stopifnot(require(knitr))
# seed = in bnec() governs Stan's sampler only. The initial values bayesnec
# generates are drawn in R, so a set.seed() here is required as well before the
# vignette is reproducible. See vignette("example1").
set.seed(17)
# Printed output wraps at getOption("width") before it reaches the page, so a
# wide table breaks across lines whatever the rendered page does. 100 fits
# summary()'s weights table and check_fit()'s columns on one line each.
options(width = 100)
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>",
  warning = FALSE,
  message = FALSE,
  fig.path = "vignette-fig-",
  fig.asp = 0.8,
  out.width = "60%",
  fig.align = "center",
  dev = "png",
  dpi = 150,
  eval = identical(Sys.getenv("NOT_CRAN"), "true")
)
```

# Overview

The other vignettes each explain one part of `bayesnec` (what the model set is,
what a `bayesmanecfit` is, how priors are built, and how to handle a two-block
response). Here we run one analysis from the data through to a reportable
estimate, taking the steps in the order in which they occur:

1. Prepare the data and choose the family.
2. Fit the candidate set.
3. Check the sampler, and screen out the models that did not sample adequately.
4. Check the fit of the models that remain.
5. Report.

A review of the priors belongs before fitting, and a sensitivity comparison
follows the fitted diagnostics in step 5. `check_priors()` and the
construction of the default priors are described in the
[*Priors*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example3.html) vignette.

## The assumptions the diagnostics test

A Bayesian fit is usually judged on convergence, although convergence is not the
only thing that can be wrong with it. `rhat()`, `check_chains()` and
`check_sampling()` ask whether the sampler explored the posterior, and `loo` and
`waic` rank the candidate equations against one another. However, none of them
asks whether the variability implied by the fitted model matches the variability
in the data. That assumption can affect a threshold estimate even when sampling
diagnostics are satisfactory.

The *NSEC* depends on it most directly. `nsec()` sets its reference at a quantile
of the posterior of the control mean, and the width of that posterior depends in
part on the dispersion estimated at the control. If a model simulates more variability at
the control than the data show, the reference is placed lower, so the curve
crosses it further along and the reported concentration is higher and less
protective. If a model simulates less variability, the reference is placed higher
and a lower concentration is returned. The direction of the error is not known in
advance, and no convergence diagnostic reveals it (this point is also made, for
the choice between constant and non-constant dispersion, in [*Single model
usage*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example1.html)). The ECx
depends on this assumption less. Although it is also measured from the control
mean, it is measured at a fixed fraction of that mean and not at a quantile of its
posterior, so an error in the variance widens or narrows the ECx interval without
relocating the reference it is measured from.

A single ratio statistic for the whole fit does not detect this kind of error.
Each family with a free dispersion parameter (i.e., `gaussian`, `Gamma`,
`negbinomial`, `Beta` and `beta_binomial`) estimates that parameter from the
residuals that such a statistic summarises. The parameter therefore absorbs the
discrepancy, so that the ratio is near 1 while the fit simulates the wrong spread
at the concentrations that determine the estimate. For this reason the check is
made within concentration groups, which is what `check_fit()` does.

## The effect of exclusion

[*Multi model usage*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example2.html)
notes that a poorly shaped model with a low predictive weight may contribute
little to a model average. A model that has not sampled adequately, however,
contributes on the basis of a posterior the sampler has not explored reliably.
Because its weight is computed from that same
posterior, the weight provides no evidence that it is safe to keep. Removing such
a model changes the candidate set. `amend()` then recomputes the weights across
the models that remain (it does not rescale the weights of those retained), and
the reported *N(S)EC* changes accordingly.

Which models a screen will remove cannot be predicted before it is run, and the
reasons for removal differ between them. An equation given too few draws, an
equation whose posterior the sampler cannot traverse, and an equation the design
does not support are all reported under one failure flag, although
the appropriate response to each is different.

## Coverage

Steps 1 to 5 are worked on one climate level of `coral_colour`, including a
comparison of default and regularising priors. Two further responses follow. The
first is a count response, for which both the choice of family and the sampler
screen take a different form. The second is a response whose coefficient of
variation is not constant, although the family assumes that it is; here the
departure is detected with a posterior check, and corrected with a term written in
the model formula.

The threshold estimates themselves (*NEC*, *NSEC*, ECx, and the model-averaged
*N(S)EC* reported when a set mixes both kinds) are defined, together with the
candidate equations, in [*Model
details*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example2b.html).

```{r libraries}
library(bayesnec)
library(ggplot2)
```

# 1. Data preparation and family choice

The `coral_colour` data record a colour score for fragments of *Acropora
millepora* after 14 days of diuron exposure under three climate scenarios
[@flores2021]. A lower proportion indicates loss of colour. Here we use the
2050 scenario. The comparison among
scenarios, and a second endpoint measured on those fragments, are described
in [*Comparing posterior predictions*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example4.html).

```{r coral-data}
data(coral_colour)
colour_2050 <- droplevels(subset(coral_colour, climate == "2050"))
with(colour_2050, table(diuron, chamber))
```

The design has five concentrations (four treatments and an untreated control:
0, 0.29, 0.96, 2.9 and 29 µg/L), with 12 fragments at each concentration.
Four fragments shared each chamber (three chambers per concentration), so there
are 15 independent chambers for assessing prediction of a new chamber. A
chamber received one concentration only; `ogl(chamber)` therefore estimates
variation among chambers at a concentration, without asking any chamber to
define a concentration-response curve on its own.

```{r coral-data-plot, out.width = "85%"}
colour_plot <- transform(colour_2050,
                         plot_dose = ifelse(diuron == 0, 0.1, diuron))
ggplot(colour_plot, aes(x = plot_dose, y = proportion)) +
  geom_point(aes(colour = chamber), alpha = 0.75, size = 2) +
  scale_x_continuous(trans = "log10",
                     breaks = sort(unique(colour_plot$plot_dose)),
                     labels = c("0", "0.29", "0.96", "2.9", "29")) +
  guides(colour = "none") +
  labs(x = expression(paste("Diuron (", mu, "g ", L^-1, ")")),
       y = "Coral colour proportion") +
  theme_classic()
```

The zero control is drawn at 0.1 µg/L on this log axis but labelled as the
recorded zero; the model uses the same substitution, stated below.

For example, the mean proportion is `r sprintf("%.3f", mean(colour_2050$proportion[colour_2050$diuron == 0]))`
in the control and `r sprintf("%.3f", mean(colour_2050$proportion[colour_2050$diuron == 29]))`
at 29 µg/L. Most of the observed decline is between 2.9 and 29 µg/L (a
ten-fold step), where the experiment has no intermediate treatment. Although
the highest treatment has a low mean colour proportion, the design has no
higher treatment to show whether the response remains there. We retain the
default `asymptote_observed = TRUE`: reaching the lower asymptote by 29 µg/L is
plausible given the response near zero, but is not verified by this design.
`bnec()` checks the change between the two highest treatments before fitting,
and we examine its message below.

The response is continuous and bounded by zero and one. An identity-linked
`Beta` family is appropriate for values in the open interval (0, 1); it also
estimates a dispersion parameter. Values recorded as zero or one would
need separate consideration, because a Beta likelihood cannot represent them
as observed. The 2050 subset has no boundary values:

```{r coral-boundaries}
range(colour_2050$proportion)
fam <- Beta(link = "identity")
```

Unlike a binomial proportion, this colour score is not a count of successes
from a known number of trials. If a Beta response includes an exact zero,
`bnec()` substitutes one tenth of the smallest positive response; an exact
one is reduced by 0.001. Such changes appear in `bnec_record()` and must be
reported, particularly if boundary values represent a real outcome. The
[*Hurdle and zero-inflated concentration-response
models*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example6.html) vignette
addresses responses with substantive zeros, while [*Single model
usage*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example1.html) compares
the supported families.

The identity link keeps `top` and any estimated lower asymptote on the colour
response scale. A logit link would fit the concentration-response equation to
the logit of the mean instead. Since `Beta` estimates its own dispersion
parameter (`phi`), a pre-fit dispersion ratio is not needed here; we check the
simulated spread within concentrations after fitting. The count response below
uses a family for which that pre-fit decision matters.

The four positive diuron concentrations span two orders of magnitude (0.29 to
29 µg/L). To fit
on a log scale we replace the zero control by 0.1 µg/L, approximately one
half-log step below the lowest treatment (0.29 µg/L), and retain the recorded
concentration separately. This substitution positions the control on the log
predictor; a threshold estimated near 0.1 µg/L would depend on that choice.
Logging also changes the equation fitted to diuron and the scale of its default
predictor prior (not just the spacing of points on a plot). We therefore state
the transformation and the control substitution together.

```{r coral-log-dose}
colour_2050$diuron_adj <- ifelse(colour_2050$diuron == 0, 0.1,
                                  colour_2050$diuron)
sort(unique(colour_2050$diuron_adj))
```

# 2. The candidate set and its priors

We first request `model = "decline"`, which includes the constant equation
`ecxflat` (a response independent of dose) and the equations for a declining
response. The data show no clear
hormetic increase, so the hormesis equations are excluded from the request.
This deliberately broad set exposes the difficulty of estimating curve shape
from five concentrations. It is a diagnostic exercise; an equation that fits
or samples poorly is not made suitable merely by assigning it a small model
weight.

Some equations in `decline` estimate a lower asymptote (`bot`), whereas others
do not. Without observations on a flat lower limb, the data may not
distinguish these forms, and `bnec()` warns when the set contains both. Whether
a response can reach zero is a property of the endpoint and not a conclusion
from this fit. A more restricted set (e.g., `models()$zero_bounded` if a zero
response is biologically attainable) should be chosen on that basis for an
analysis intended to support a substantive toxicity estimate.

The family and logged predictor also rule out equations before sampling:
`neclin` and `ecxlin` can predict means outside the Beta family's bounds under
an identity link, and `ecxsigm` cannot evaluate its fractional power at the
negative logged control. `get_priors()` returns entries only for equations that
remain valid (see [*Model
details*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example2b.html) for
their equations and `?models` for the model groups).

```{r coral-priors}
coral_formula <- proportion ~ crf(log(diuron_adj), model = "decline") +
  ogl(chamber)
prior_default <- get_priors(coral_formula, data = colour_2050, family = fam,
                            prior_type = "uninformative")
prior_regular <- get_priors(coral_formula, data = colour_2050, family = fam,
                            prior_type = "regularizing")
names(prior_default)
```

The default and regularising priors differ for several parameters. For
example, the `ec50` prior is defined on log concentration (and returned on the
measured concentration scale), while the `top` prior is on the response scale.
Their entries for `ecxwb1p3` can be inspected before
fitting:

```{r coral-prior-entries}
selected_priors <- function(x, label) {
  p <- as.data.frame(x[["ecxwb1p3"]])
  p <- p[p$nlpar %in% c("ec50", "top"),
         c("nlpar", "prior", "lb", "ub")]
  data.frame(prior_type = label, p, row.names = NULL)
}
rbind(selected_priors(prior_default, "uninformative"),
      selected_priors(prior_regular, "regularizing"))
```

These are distinct assumptions about the plausible range of the midpoint and
upper response. Sampling may be more stable under narrower priors on sparse data,
but they can also narrow the reported interval when the likelihood provides
little information. We fit both choices with identical data and sampling
settings below. [*Priors*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example3.html)
explains how the entries are constructed and how to inspect their relation to
the posterior.

## Fitting the full set

`bnec()` uses four chains and 10,000 iterations per chain by default (40,000
iterations in total). Its
warmup rule discards 8,000 iterations per chain, leaving 8,000 post-warmup
draws across the four chains. We use those defaults for both prior choices;
the seed controls Stan sampling and `set.seed()` controls the initial values
generated in R. The vignette precompile selects the `cmdstanr` backend through
`options(brms.backend = "cmdstanr")`. Set that option before running these
chunks in a separate R session to use the same backend; otherwise `brms`
defaults to `rstan`, and the diagnostic counts may differ.

```{r coral-fit-default, results = "hide", message = TRUE}
set.seed(228)
fit_default <- bnec(coral_formula, data = colour_2050, family = fam,
                    prior_type = "uninformative", seed = 228, refresh = 0)
```

The message compares mean colour at 2.9 and 29 µg/L. A decline between those
levels is clear, but this check cannot tell whether the curve flattens at
29 µg/L or continues to fall beyond it. We keep the default declaration because
the final response is near the lower bound, while recognising that one
high-dose group cannot establish a plateau. Setting
`asymptote_observed = FALSE` would be a different prior assumption: it changes
the `bot` prior and removes the upper bound of the `nec` and `ec50` priors. The
warning calls for examining that assumption, not changing it automatically.

The record separates equations excluded before fitting from those attempted
and those that failed during fitting. It also records any values changed to
make the response compatible with the family. Here the 2050 colour values are
inside the Beta support, so no response substitution is expected.

```{r coral-record}
record_default <- bnec_record(fit_default)
record_default$excluded
record_default$attempted
record_default$substitutions
names(failed_models(fit_default))
```

The requested set is partitioned into `attempted` and `excluded$model`;
an attempted equation that did not fit remains in `attempted`.
`failed_models()` also retains the priors and initial values used for such
an equation, which are useful when investigating the failure. A different
initialisation or prior may allow a fit to start, although that does not show
that the resulting posterior is identified by these concentrations.

# 3. Sampler diagnostics and screening

An attempted equation that produces draws must still be checked. The package
screen uses maximum Rhat ≤ 1.01, minimum effective sample size (ESS) ≥ 400,
and at most ten divergent transitions. The Rhat and ESS cutoffs follow the
recommendations of @Vehtari2021; ten divergences is a `bayesnec` convention,
while Stan regards any divergence as evidence that some posterior geometry
was not traversed. A screen result is specific to the data, priors, sampler
settings and seed.

```{r coral-sampling-default}
sampling_default <- check_sampling(fit_default)
sampling_default
```

The `failed` column is `TRUE` if any cutoff is missed. For example, more
iterations may raise a low ESS where there are no divergences. Divergences describe a
different problem: additional draws visit that difficult geometry more
often, so increasing `iter` by itself is unlikely to remove them. A higher
`adapt_delta` may reduce them, although it cannot identify a lower asymptote that the
design did not observe. An equation that never initialises is recorded by
`failed_models()` and has no sampler diagnostics to screen. For example, a
chain rejected at initialisation cannot contribute to an ESS calculation.

The decision uses `min_ess` (an absolute count), not `min_ess_ratio` (a measure
of sampling efficiency). A model can meet the count after many draws while
still sampling inefficiently, so both columns are worth reading. The 1.01
Rhat convention also applies to `rhat()` and `summary()`.

The ESS floor and divergence allowance are absolute counts. At a fixed
divergence rate a longer run will record more divergences, while it may also
provide enough draws for an ESS above 400. Thus a comparison between runs
needs their chain counts, iterations and warmup beside the diagnostic table.
If Stan also warns about maximum tree depth, `max_treedepth` is the
relevant control to examine (see `?check_sampling`); it does not add
information about an unsampled concentration interval.

An equation failing Rhat or ESS without exceeding the divergence allowance
should be refitted under the original prior with more iterations before it is
excluded. The next check makes this requirement explicit. If any such equation
appears in a later run, the analysis stops here so it can be refitted and
screened again; changing the prior would not test whether the original run
was simply too short.

```{r coral-retry-gate}
retry_only <- function(tab) {
  tab[tab$failed & tab$n_divergent <= 10 &
        (tab$max_rhat > 1.01 | tab$min_ess < 400), ]
}
retry_only(sampling_default)
if (nrow(retry_only(sampling_default))) {
  stop("Refit the Rhat/ESS-only failures with more iterations before screening.")
}
```

With only five concentrations, the distinction between curve parameters is
most informative. `models()` returns their number for each fitted equation:

```{r coral-parameter-count}
data.frame(
  model = sampling_default$model,
  curve_parameters = vapply(sampling_default$model, function(m)
    length(names(show_params(m)[[1]]$pforms)), integer(1)),
  failed = sampling_default$failed,
  row.names = NULL
)
```

The four and five parameter equations (e.g., `nec4param` and `ecxll5`) require
the lower limb or its shape to be estimated largely across the unsampled
interval from 2.9 to 29 µg/L. Their poor sampling may therefore have a design
explanation, although the
diagnostics do not prove which parameter is responsible. We remove
equations that miss the stated cutoffs and retain a record of the reasons:

```{r coral-screen-default, message = TRUE}
screened_default <- screen_models(fit_default)
```

`screen_models()` recomputes the model weights over the equations that pass.
For example, the flat equation may remain because it samples well, even if it
has almost no predictive weight. Screening asks whether posterior draws are reliable; it
does not select equations for good fit to the observed response. Its messages
name each excluded equation and the failed cutoffs; we leave them visible for
the analysis record. If all fitted equations fail, the function stops with
the reasons instead of returning a model average.

## Chamber-level model weights

The four fragments in each chamber are related observations (a grouping
omitted from an observation-wise leave-out calculation). Since
`ogl(chamber)` is in the formula, the default model weights leave out a whole
chamber at a time. Pareto *k* assesses whether the importance-sampling
approximation is reliable for each left-out chamber; an equation with a high
weight and *k* above 0.7 needs particular attention.

The `wi` column of `summary()` is the share of the model-averaged prediction
assigned to each equation (the weights sum to one). They are computed from
leave-one-chamber-out scores by the default pseudo-BMA method. A small weight
does not validate a poorly sampled equation, which is why we screen before
interpreting this table. The separate Pareto section of `check_fit()` names
each equation's weight, number of chambers with *k* above 0.7 and largest *k*.
It then lists affected chambers. The concentration rows test posterior
predictive fit. The full Pareto table is available through
`attr(cf_default, "pareto")`.

```{r coral-pareto-check}
cf_default <- check_fit(screened_default, ndraws = 100)
print(cf_default)
k_default <- attr(cf_default, "pareto")
if (anyNA(k_default$n_high_k)) stop("Pareto diagnostics are unavailable")
high_k_default <- k_default[k_default$n_high_k > 0, , drop = FALSE]
```

If a contributing equation has a high *k*, `amend(x, loo_controls =
list(refit = TRUE))` refits it without each affected chamber and recomputes
the weights. This check belongs after screening, because weights for models
whose posterior was not sampled adequately should not be interpreted.

```{r coral-reloo-default, message = TRUE}
set.seed(228)
screened_default <- if (nrow(high_k_default)) {
  amend(screened_default, loo_controls = list(refit = TRUE))
} else {
  screened_default
}
round(summary(screened_default)$mod_weights[, "wi", drop = FALSE], 3)
```

## Regularising priors and screening

The concentration gap also limits how precisely a no-effect concentration can
be estimated. To assess the contribution of the prior, we fit the full
candidate request with `prior_type = "regularizing"` and screen it by the
same rules. Sampling may become more reliable for some equations and less so for
others; regularisation is
not a substitute for checking each fit.

```{r coral-fit-regular, results = "hide"}
set.seed(228)
fit_regular <- bnec(coral_formula, data = colour_2050, family = fam,
                    prior_type = "regularizing", seed = 228, refresh = 0)
```

```{r coral-sampling-regular}
sampling_regular <- check_sampling(fit_regular)
sampling_regular
names(failed_models(fit_regular))
retry_only(sampling_regular)
if (nrow(retry_only(sampling_regular))) {
  stop("Refit the Rhat/ESS-only failures with more iterations before screening.")
}
```

For a direct comparison, the following table gives maximum Rhat, minimum
ESS and divergent transitions for each valid equation under both priors.
"Did not fit" is distinct from a fitted equation that missed the sampler
cutoffs.

```{r coral-sampling-comparison}
diagnostic_label <- function(tab, model) {
  i <- match(model, tab$model)
  if (is.na(i)) return("did not fit")
  x <- tab[i, ]
  sprintf("%.3f / %.0f / %d%s", x$max_rhat, x$min_ess,
          x$n_divergent, if (x$failed) " (fail)" else " (pass)")
}
data.frame(
  model = names(prior_default),
  uninformative = vapply(names(prior_default), function(m)
    diagnostic_label(sampling_default, m), character(1)),
  regularizing = vapply(names(prior_default), function(m)
    diagnostic_label(sampling_regular, m), character(1)),
  row.names = NULL
)
```

For this dataset the effect of the regularising choice differs among equations.
In particular, a reduction in divergences for one equation
cannot be taken as evidence that the whole set now samples well. We screen
again and check the groupwise Pareto *k* values before interpreting weights.

```{r coral-screen-regular, message = TRUE}
screened_regular <- screen_models(fit_regular)
cf_regular <- check_fit(screened_regular, ndraws = 100)
print(cf_regular)
k_regular <- attr(cf_regular, "pareto")
if (anyNA(k_regular$n_high_k)) stop("Pareto diagnostics are unavailable")
high_k_regular <- k_regular[k_regular$n_high_k > 0, , drop = FALSE]
```

Where a high *k* occurs, the refit below replaces the approximate
leave-one-chamber-out score for that chamber. The code also works if no chamber
exceeds 0.7 in a later run, in which case no refit is needed.

```{r coral-reloo, message = TRUE}
set.seed(228)
screened_regular <- if (nrow(high_k_regular)) {
  amend(screened_regular, loo_controls = list(refit = TRUE))
} else {
  screened_regular
}
round(summary(screened_regular)$mod_weights[, "wi", drop = FALSE], 3)
```

## Chain mixing and fitted curves

The regularising prior gives the final candidate set for this analysis. We first
inspect chain traces for its highest-weighted equation, then compare the curves
of every equation retained after screening on the measured concentration scale.

```{r coral-chains, out.width = "90%"}
best_regular <- pull_best(screened_regular)
check_chains(best_regular)
```

The displayed equation is an example. Before reporting a model average we
would inspect the traces of each retained equation; `check_chains()` can write
one page per model to a PDF for review:

```{r coral-all-chains, eval = FALSE}
check_chains(screened_regular, filename = "coral_2050_chains")
```

In the trace panel draws from each chain should overlap throughout a stable range;
the autocorrelation panel shows how quickly draws cease to depend on earlier
draws. A satisfactory Rhat does not replace inspection of these plots.
`pull_best()` selects the highest-weighted equation; the model weights above
show how much it contributes to the average.

```{r coral-curves, fig.asp = 1, out.width = "95%"}
autoplot(screened_regular, model = screened_regular$success_models,
         average = FALSE, nec = FALSE) +
  scale_x_continuous(trans = "log10",
                     breaks = sort(unique(colour_2050$diuron_adj))) +
  labs(x = expression(paste("Diuron (", mu, "g ", L^-1, ")")),
       y = "Coral colour proportion")
```

Curves can differ substantially through the interval from 2.9 to 29 µg/L,
where no intermediate response was
observed. The model-averaged curve used for inference is shown at the end of
the analysis.

# 4. Fit diagnostics

Sampler diagnostics concern the posterior draws, whereas `check_fit()` compares
observed and simulated response within each concentration. We apply it to the
screened regularising set used for inference, so that every retained equation
is checked. The control is shown separately because the N(S)EC and ECx use its
fitted mean as a reference.

```{r coral-checkfit}
cf_regular <- check_fit(screened_regular)
round_cols <- function(x, digits = 3) {
  x[vapply(x, is.numeric, logical(1))] <-
    lapply(x[vapply(x, is.numeric, logical(1))], round, digits)
  x
}
fit_cols <- c("model", "wi", "group", "obs_mean", "sim_mean",
              "obs_sd", "sim_sd", "sd_ratio", "ppp_mean", "ppp_sd")
round_cols(as.data.frame(cf_regular[cf_regular$control, fit_cols]))
flagged_regular <- cf_regular$ppp_mean < 0.05 | cf_regular$ppp_mean > 0.95 |
  cf_regular$ppp_sd < 0.05 | cf_regular$ppp_sd > 0.95
round_cols(as.data.frame(cf_regular[flagged_regular, fit_cols]))
```

The posterior predictive p-value is the fraction of simulated group statistics
at least as large as the observed statistic. Values outside [0.05, 0.95]
identify concentrations worth inspecting; with twelve fragments in three
chambers at each concentration, a flag is not by itself a reason to exclude an
equation. The displayed rows also show whether a discrepancy concerns the mean
or the spread, including at the control.

For a closer view, we plot the highest-weighted retained equation. The points
are observed group statistics and the bars are 95% intervals of statistics
simulated from that equation.

```{r coral-checkfit-plot, out.width = "85%"}
cf_best <- cf_regular[cf_regular$model == best_regular$model, , drop = FALSE]
plot(cf_best)
```

A point may remain inside a plotted 95% interval even when its posterior
predictive p-value crosses the [0.05, 0.95] flag threshold. The table uses the
p-values, while the plot shows the direction and size of a discrepancy.

# 5. Prior sensitivity and reporting

Model averages over both screened sets differ in their prior choices
and the equations that survived. To isolate prior sensitivity in the reported
interval, we use the smooth equations that passed under both priors. The
specific passing equations may depend on the sampler backend and seed, so
we identify their intersection from the two diagnostic tables. Where several
remain, weights are recomputed for the common candidate set. Its
leave-one-chamber-out diagnostics are checked against the 0.7 cutoff for each fit.

```{r coral-common-set}
shared_models <- intersect(names(models(screened_default)),
                           names(models(screened_regular)))
shared_models <- setdiff(shared_models[grepl("^ecx", shared_models)], "ecxflat")
if (!length(shared_models)) {
  stop("No common passing decline equation is available for the prior comparison.")
}
shared_models
make_common <- function(x, selected) {
  fits <- lapply(selected, function(m) pull_out(x, model = m))
  if (length(fits) == 1L) fits[[1]] else do.call(c, fits)
}
set.seed(228)
common_default <- make_common(screened_default, shared_models)
set.seed(228)
common_regular <- make_common(screened_regular, shared_models)
attr(check_fit(common_default, ndraws = 100), "pareto")
attr(check_fit(common_regular, ndraws = 100), "pareto")
correct_k <- function(x) {
  p <- attr(check_fit(x, ndraws = 100), "pareto")
  if (anyNA(p$n_high_k)) stop("Pareto diagnostics are unavailable")
  if (any(p$n_high_k > 0)) {
    amend(x, loo_controls = list(refit = TRUE))
  } else {
    x
  }
}
set.seed(228)
common_default <- correct_k(common_default)
set.seed(228)
common_regular <- correct_k(common_regular)
```

```{r coral-prior-effect}
rbind(uninformative = nsec(common_default),
      regularizing = nsec(common_regular))
```

Both rows estimate the NSEC from the `shared_models` set with identical
observations (i.e., with the candidate set fixed). A narrower interval under
the regularising prior may therefore reflect its stronger assumptions in
addition to information from the data;
it cannot be presented as precision supplied solely by the experiment. The
control fit should also be checked under both choices, because the NSEC
reference depends on it:

```{r coral-prior-control}
control_comparison <- function(x, label) {
  d <- as.data.frame(check_fit(x))
  if (!"model" %in% names(d)) d$model <- names(models(x))[1]
  d <- d[d$control, c("model", "obs_mean", "sim_mean", "obs_sd",
                        "sim_sd", "ppp_sd")]
  data.frame(prior_type = label, d, row.names = NULL)
}
rbind(control_comparison(common_default, "uninformative"),
      control_comparison(common_regular, "regularizing"))
```

The comparison fixes the candidate set, so the difference between rows can be
attributed to the priors within those equations. The final regularising set can
contain additional equations. Its model-averaged estimate also reflects their
weights, and should not be interpreted as a pure prior effect. The prior
construction and density checks are described in [*Priors*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example3.html).

## Final model-averaged fit

We use the screened regularising set for the illustrative inference. The plot
shows its model-averaged curve and 95% credible band on the measured diuron
scale; the individual retained curves were shown above.

```{r coral-final-plot, fig.asp = 0.75, out.width = "85%"}
autoplot(screened_regular, nec = FALSE) +
  scale_x_continuous(trans = "log10",
                     breaks = sort(unique(colour_2050$diuron_adj))) +
  labs(x = expression(paste("Diuron (", mu, "g ", L^-1, ")")),
       y = "Coral colour proportion")
```

The table gives the median and equal-tailed 95% credible interval for EC10,
EC50 and NSEC (µg/L). Here `nsec()` uses the same control-reference definition
for every retained equation. Where a set mixes threshold and smooth equations,
`nec()` instead returns a model-averaged mixture of NEC and NSEC draws, labelled
N(S)EC by `summary()`.

```{r coral-final-estimates, warning = TRUE}
coral_estimates <- rbind(
  EC10 = ecx(screened_regular, ecx_val = 10)[c("Q50", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")],
  EC50 = ecx(screened_regular, ecx_val = 50)[c("Q50", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")],
  NSEC = nsec(screened_regular)[c("Q50", "Q2.5", "Q97.5")]
)
round(coral_estimates, 3)
```

These values depend on the control substitution to 0.1 µg/L before logging,
`asymptote_observed = TRUE`, the candidate set and its regularising priors. In
particular, only one observed concentration has a mean near the lower response
bound. The package's flatness message and the fixed-set prior comparison above
should accompany the estimates. The ECx values use the default absolute
response scale; `type = "range"` would instead measure the decline between the
fitted upper and lower response levels. A flat equation may have no finite ECx
crossing within the tested range, so any censored draws also need to be
reported.

# A count response {#a-count-response}

When the response is a count out of a known number of trials, both the family
question and the sampler screen take a different form. The family question becomes
a question about dispersion, and the sampler screen can return an outcome that
more sampling does not fix. Here we use the `nassarius` data to demonstrate both.

The data are survival counts for the tropical marine snail *Nassarius dorsatus*
exposed to each of four contaminants, recorded per individual within replicate
tanks [@trenfield2016]. `?nassarius` documents the columns and the provenance of
the response.

```{r nass-data}
data(nassarius)
a <- nassarius[nassarius$contaminant == "A", ]
table(a$record, a$alive)
```

The `record` column is important. A death is recorded as `alive = 0`, but most of
those zeros were not read from the source file, because a treatment in which
nothing survived was not written down at all, and its rows were reinstated from
the test design. `record` gives how the fate of each snail was established
(`measured`, `sentinel_code`, `blank_rep`, `absent_tank` or `absent_dose`), and
the last two of these are the reinstated records (30 of the 38 deaths in
contaminant A). Each dose at which any snail died is largely or wholly
reconstructed, so the descending limb that determines the reported estimate is
derived almost entirely from reinstated records. This is a property of the data
and not of `bayesnec`, and it should be stated in the methods section of any
analysis based on these data. The corresponding sensitivity analysis, on
contaminant B, is run in [*Hurdle and zero-inflated concentration-response
models*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example6.html).

Survival is binary per individual, so the natural unit for a
concentration-response model is the tank: the number alive out of the number
exposed.

```{r nass-aggregate}
surv <- aggregate(alive ~ dose + tank + contaminant,
                  data = nassarius, FUN = function(x) c(suc = sum(x),
                                                        tot = length(x)))
surv <- do.call(data.frame, surv)
names(surv)[names(surv) == "alive.suc"] <- "suc"
names(surv)[names(surv) == "alive.tot"] <- "tot"
surv <- surv[surv$contaminant == "A", ]
head(surv)
```

A concentration series laid out as a dilution ladder is read on a logarithmic
axis, which is what spaces its steps evenly. However, a logarithm is undefined at
zero, so the control has to be given a plotting position. Here we use half the
lowest tested dose, marked on the axis as the control and not as a number, so that
nothing on the figure claims that the control was a dose it was not.

This substitution is made only for the figure. The fit uses the recorded dose,
including the control, and all estimates are reported in those units. The plotting
position and the recorded dose can differ safely because of the control convention
in `bayesnec`, which takes the control to be the lowest value of the predictor,
whatever that value is (as set out in step 4).

```{r exmp9-nass-data}
ctrl_pos <- min(surv$dose[surv$dose > 0]) / 2
surv$dose_plot <- ifelse(surv$dose == 0, ctrl_pos, surv$dose)
dose_breaks <- c(ctrl_pos, 0.01, 0.04, 0.16, 0.63, 2.5, 20)
# Most tanks sit at exactly 1 or exactly 0, so the points overplot and the
# number of tanks at each dose cannot be read. The vertical jitter is a display
# device and the seed is set so the figure is reproducible; no value shown is
# the value recorded, and the fit uses the recorded proportions.
set.seed(17)
ggplot(surv, aes(x = dose_plot, y = suc / tot)) +
  geom_point(size = 2, alpha = 0.6,
             position = position_jitter(width = 0, height = 0.015)) +
  # Axis spacing only: the transform is applied to the scale, so the spacing is
  # logarithmic while the labels stay in recorded units. Transforming the breaks
  # instead would relabel the axis without respacing it.
  scale_x_continuous(transform = "log", breaks = dose_breaks,
                     labels = c("0\n(control)", "0.01", "0.04", "0.16", "0.63",
                                "2.5", "20")) +
  labs(x = "Dose", y = "Proportion surviving") +
  theme_classic()
```

Survival is at or near complete for most of the series, and falls away over the
top two doses (2.5 and 20). Nine of the eleven doses have survival at or above
0.875, and the whole of the decline occurs between 1.25 and 2.5, with no dose
tested in between. The logarithmic axis is what makes that gap visible, because on
a linear axis the nine doses up to 1.25 would occupy the leftmost six per cent of
the plot and overlap into a single stack.

## The dispersion screen

The default family for successes out of trials is `binomial`, which fixes the
variance at $np(1-p)$ (where $n$ is the number of trials and $p$ the probability
of success). However, toxicity data often vary by more than that, for any of
several reasons: animals within a replicate share conditions, so the effective
sample size is smaller than the number of individuals; individuals differ in
susceptibility, so the probability of survival is not one number but a
distribution; and covariates that were not measured contribute similarly. The
`beta_binomial` family writes the second of these down explicitly (`p` varies
between units, drawn from a beta distribution), and in practice it accommodates
the others as well.

This correction works in one direction only. Mixing `p` over a beta distribution
can only *add* variance, so `beta_binomial` addresses over-dispersion, but not the
opposite case. A screen pointing below 1 therefore indicates something about the
fit, the design or the data-generating process, and not about the family.

We first fit the `binomial` family and inspect dispersion across the fitted
equations. `dispersion()` compares the observed and simulated sums of squared
Pearson residuals for each posterior draw. Its median and 95% interval describe
the ratio, while `P(>1)` gives the proportion of draws with a ratio above one.
The diagnostic depends on curve shape: a model that fits the mean response
poorly may have a large ratio even when the family is suitable. We therefore
interpret it alongside the fitted curves and sampler diagnostics. The
`beta_binomial` family would be a candidate if well-fitting equations indicated
over-dispersion.

## Divergent transitions and the design

We fit the full `decline` set with the default sampling settings, as in the coral
analysis. The binomial family is provisional until we examine dispersion in the
equations that sample adequately.

```{r nass-fit, results = "hide"}
fit_nass <- bnec(suc | trials(tot) ~ crf(dose, model = "decline"),
                 data = surv, family = "binomial", seed = 17)
```

```{r nass-sampling}
nass_sampling <- check_sampling(fit_nass)
nass_sampling
```

```{r nass-screen-models, message = TRUE}
nass_screened <- screen_models(fit_nass)
```

The dispersion estimate is available for every equation that fitted, including
those excluded by the sampler screen. We show the latter only to examine whether
a poor curve shape might inflate the statistic; their dispersion estimates are
not evidence for selecting a family.

```{r nass-dispersion-by-model}
nass_disp <- fit_nass$mod_stats[, c("model", "dispersion_Estimate",
                                   "dispersion_P_over_1")]
nass_disp$failed_screen <- nass_sampling$failed[
  match(nass_disp$model, nass_sampling$model)]
nass_disp$dispersion_Estimate <- signif(nass_disp$dispersion_Estimate, 3)
nass_disp$dispersion_P_over_1 <- signif(nass_disp$dispersion_P_over_1, 3)
nass_disp
```

```{r exmp9-nass-all-curves, fig.asp = 0.75, out.width = "90%"}
autoplot(nass_screened, model = nass_screened$success_models,
         average = FALSE, nec = FALSE)
```

These panels show every equation retained after screening. We use them with the
dispersion table to assess whether a high ratio is associated with poor fit to
the mean, including the flat equation. Dispersion estimates from screened-out
equations remain unreliable because their posterior sampling failed. The
retained equations and their weights are shown separately:

```{r nass-retained-summary}
nass_summary <- summary(nass_screened)$mod_weights
lead_nass <- rownames(nass_summary)[which.max(nass_summary[, "wi"])]
round(nass_summary[, c("wi", "dispersion_Estimate",
                       "dispersion_P_over_1"), drop = FALSE], 3)
```

The highest-weighted equation, `r lead_nass`, has weight
`r sprintf("%.3f", nass_summary[lead_nass, "wi"])`, a median dispersion ratio
of `r sprintf("%.2f", nass_summary[lead_nass, "dispersion_Estimate"])` and
`P(>1) = r sprintf("%.3f", nass_summary[lead_nass, "dispersion_P_over_1"])`.
By comparison, the flat equation has a ratio of
`r sprintf("%.2f", nass_summary["ecxflat", "dispersion_Estimate"])` and weight
`r signif(nass_summary["ecxflat", "wi"], 2)`; its inability to describe the
decline may contribute to that large ratio. We retain `binomial` for this
worked analysis, while recognising that the leading equation's ratio also
suggests some extra variation. With `r nrow(surv)` tanks across
`r length(unique(surv$dose))` doses, a `beta_binomial` fit would be a useful
sensitivity check before using this estimate for a decision.

The 2050 colour analysis also showed that a broad candidate set can contain
equations with divergent transitions at the default sampling length. In this
count response, most of the `decline` set still fails, with divergent
transitions in the hundreds and thousands for each failing equation. These designs
leave different parts of the response curve unresolved.

Increasing iterations alone is unlikely to resolve these divergences. The
cutoff is an absolute count over post-warmup draws, so at an unchanged
divergence rate a longer run would record more of them. Their occurrence
calls for examining the posterior geometry and the equations.

No dose was tested between 1.25 and 2.5, where the observed response changes
sharply. The sparse coverage may contribute to difficult posterior geometry for
equations that estimate a threshold or steep midpoint, although the sampler
diagnostics do not identify its cause. Raising `adapt_delta` can reduce
divergences, but cannot supply an observation within that gap.

The concentration gap should therefore be reported with any model-based
no-effect estimate.

```{r nass-estimate}
nsec(nass_screened)
```

The estimate should be read beside the gap between 1.25 and 2.5, where no dose
was tested, and with the equations excluded by the sampler screen. Its credible
interval is a model-based summary; it does not simply reproduce the boundaries
of the untested interval. The fitted set illustrates the uncertainty there.

```{r exmp9-nass-autoplot, fig.asp = 0.72, out.width = "80%"}
# autoplot() returns the predictor as recorded, and the control is a true zero,
# which a logarithmic axis cannot place. The control is given the same plotting
# position the raw-data figure used, and the prediction grid is clipped there.
nass_plot <- ggbnec_data(nass_screened)
nass_plot$x_e[nass_plot$x_e == 0] <- ctrl_pos
nass_plot$x_r[nass_plot$x_r == 0] <- ctrl_pos
nass_plot <- nass_plot[is.na(nass_plot$x_e) | nass_plot$x_e >= ctrl_pos, ]
nass_nsec <- nsec(nass_screened)

ggplot() +
  geom_polygon(data = subset(nass_plot, !is.na(y_ci)), aes(x = x_e, y = y_ci),
               fill = "grey80", alpha = 0.7) +
  geom_line(data = subset(nass_plot, !is.na(y_e)), aes(x = x_e, y = y_e),
            linetype = 2) +
  geom_point(data = subset(nass_plot, !is.na(x_r)), aes(x = x_r, y = y_r),
             size = 1.6, alpha = 0.6) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = nass_nsec[1], colour = "grey40") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = nass_nsec[2:3], colour = "grey40", linetype = 3) +
  scale_x_continuous(transform = "log", breaks = dose_breaks,
                     labels = c("0\n(control)", "0.01", "0.04", "0.16", "0.63",
                                "2.5", "20")) +
  labs(x = "Dose", y = "Proportion surviving") +
  theme_classic()
```

The dashed line is the model-averaged prediction over the retained equations, and
the grey band is its 95% credible interval. The band is narrow wherever a dose was
tested, and opens across the interval from `r max(surv$dose[surv$dose < 2])` to
`r min(surv$dose[surv$dose > 2])`, where none was, because the curve has to fall
somewhere in that interval and the data do not indicate where. The solid line is
the model-averaged *N(S)EC*, and the dotted lines are its bounds, at
`r sprintf("%.2f", nass_nsec[2])` and `r sprintf("%.2f", nass_nsec[3])`, which lie
on either side of the lower end of that untested interval. The axis is the one
used for the raw-data figure, so the control keeps its marked position, and the
nine doses at or below `r max(surv$dose[surv$dose < 2])` are legible instead of
stacked.

# A non-constant coefficient of variation {#a-non-constant-coefficient-of-variation}

In step 4 we compared the fitted spread with the observed spread within each
concentration group. The 2050 colour fit had a local discrepancy at 0.96 µg/L.
Here we work through a third response whose coefficient of variation changes
systematically across concentrations, although the fitted family initially
assumes that it is constant. No convergence diagnostic detects this departure.

In each family with a free dispersion parameter (e.g., `Gamma`, `Beta` or
`negbinomial`), that parameter is constant along the series unless the formula
specifies otherwise. For a `Gamma` family under the identity link, a constant
`shape` is a constant coefficient of variation (i.e., the standard deviation of a
replicate is a fixed multiple of the fitted mean at each concentration). Where
replicate variation does not behave in this way, a single `shape` is estimated as
a compromise between the low-concentration and high-concentration ends of the
series, and does not describe either end.

## The assay

The `lum31` data contain the results of acute copper and zinc toxicity tests run
on the Lum-31 bioluminescent bacterial assay [@luter2025]. A suspension of marine
bacteria is dosed in a microplate well and its light output is read after a fixed
exposure, so the response is luminescence in relative light units.

Here we work through one plate of the copper series at 15 minutes (plate
`Cu 19Mar24 Rep1B`). A plate is a complete test (i.e., eleven concentrations by
four replicate wells). Because the plate reader sets its gain for each read,
plates are not on a common vertical scale, and taking a single plate avoids that
difference instead of modelling it. The group-level terms that handle the whole
assay, and the left-censoring that the blank correction requires, are described in
[*Grouping and factor
covariates*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example8.html).

```{r lum31-data}
data(lum31)
cu <- droplevels(subset(lum31, plate == "Cu 19Mar24 Rep1B" & minutes == 15))
c(wells = nrow(cu), concentrations = length(unique(cu$conc)),
  at_the_floor = sum(cu$censoring != "none"))
```

This plate is one of two in the arm of
`r length(unique(subset(lum31, toxicant == "Cu" & minutes == 15)$plate))` with the
fewest readings at the floor of the instrument (one of `r nrow(cu)`). A reading
held at a bound contributes a smaller spread than the well would have shown, so a
plate with many such readings would understate the very quantity examined in this
section.

Each concentration on one plate has four wells, so a coefficient of variation
computed within a concentration involves no curve, and cannot be inflated by any
lack of fit in the mean.

```{r lum31-cv}
cv_obs <- tapply(cu$rlu_cens, cu$conc, function(z) sd(z) / mean(z))
round(cv_obs, 3)
```

The coefficient of variation is between `r sprintf("%.3f", min(cv_obs[1:5]))` and
`r sprintf("%.3f", max(cv_obs[1:5]))` over the five lowest concentrations, then
rises without interruption to `r sprintf("%.3f", max(cv_obs))` at
`r sprintf("%.2g", as.numeric(names(cv_obs))[which.max(cv_obs)])` mg/L, and eases
to `r sprintf("%.3f", cv_obs[length(cv_obs)])` at the bottom of the series. The
largest of these values is `r round(max(cv_obs) / cv_obs[1])` times the control
value, whereas a `Gamma` fitted with one `shape` returns a single value for all of
them.

## Fitting and screening the set

The copper response falls from its control, so `decline` is the candidate set. The
zinc series in this dataset rises above its control at low concentrations and also
needs the hormesis equations, which is why `all` is fitted to it in *Grouping and
factor covariates*.

```{r disp-const, results = "hide"}
gfam <- Gamma(link = "identity")
set_const <- bnec(rlu_cens | cens(censoring) ~ crf(log(conc), model = "decline"),
                  data = cu, family = gfam, seed = 17)
```

```{r disp-const-screen, message = TRUE}
screened_const <- screen_models(set_const)
best_const <- pull_best(screened_const)
```

```{r exmp9-disp-autoplot-const, fig.asp = 0.72, out.width = "80%"}
copper_axis <- list(
  scale_x_continuous(transform = "log", breaks = c(0.003, 0.03, 0.3, 3),
                     labels = function(x) signif(x, 2)),
  scale_y_continuous(labels = function(x) {
    format(x, big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)
  }),
  labs(x = expression(paste("Copper (mg ", L^-1, ")")),
       y = "Relative light units")
)
autoplot(screened_const) + copper_axis
```

The curve is the model-averaged prediction over the retained equations, shown with
its 95% credible band and with the model-averaged *N(S)EC* marked. The mean is
described well, with the response remaining above
`r round(100 * with(aggregate(rlu_cens ~ conc, cu, mean), rlu_cens[4] / rlu_cens[1]))`
per cent of its control value up to `r sprintf("%.2g", sort(unique(cu$conc))[4])`
mg/L, and falling to below one per cent by
`r sprintf("%.2g", sort(unique(cu$conc))[7])`.

The credible band describes uncertainty in the fitted mean. Its width near the
control motivates a closer check of the local response variation, but comparing
it with the range of four wells is not a test of the Gamma variance assumption.
We use `check_fit()` for that comparison below.

`r length(models()$decline)` equations were requested. `bnec_record()` shows that
`r nrow(bnec_record(set_const)$excluded)` were excluded before fitting and
`r length(failed_models(set_const))` failed to fit, which on `r nrow(cu)`
observations is the five-parameter equation, and the screen removed
`r sum(check_sampling(set_const)$failed)` more (all three mechanisms are covered
in steps 2 and 3).

## Checking variability by concentration

A constant Gamma `shape` implies a constant coefficient of variation. We use
`check_fit()` to compare the observed spread within each concentration with
replicate responses simulated from the highest-weighted equation. The model-set
check below extends this comparison to every retained equation.

```{r checkfit-const}
cf_const <- check_fit(best_const)
cf_const[, c("group", "n", "obs_sd", "sim_sd", "sd_ratio", "ppp_sd", "control")]
```

`sd_ratio` is the observed spread divided by the simulated spread, so a value of 1
indicates agreement. Here it is between
`r sprintf("%.2f", min(cf_const$sd_ratio[1:5]))` and
`r sprintf("%.2f", max(cf_const$sd_ratio[1:5]))` over the five lowest
concentrations, then rises to a maximum of
`r sprintf("%.2f", max(cf_const$sd_ratio))` at
`r sprintf("%.2g", as.numeric(cf_const$group)[which.max(cf_const$sd_ratio)])`
mg/L, and `r sum(cf_const$ppp_sd > 0.95 | cf_const$ppp_sd < 0.05)` of the
`r nrow(cf_const)` groups are flagged. The fit simulates about
`r round(1 / cf_const$sd_ratio[cf_const$control])` times the spread observed at
the control, and about `r round(max(cf_const$sd_ratio))` times too little where
the ratio is largest. A single `shape` cannot do both, which is what a constant
coefficient of variation requires of it.

## The dispersion sub-model

`disp("power")` writes `log(shape)` as a linear function of `log(mu)`, so that the
dispersion parameter becomes a function of the fitted mean instead of a constant.
The variance functions available, the families to which each applies, and what the
estimated exponent implies for a given family are described in [*Single model
usage*](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example1.html).

```{r disp-power, results = "hide"}
set_power <- bnec(rlu_cens | cens(censoring) ~ crf(log(conc), model = "decline") +
                    disp("power"),
                  data = cu, family = gfam, seed = 17)
```

```{r disp-power-screen, message = TRUE}
screened_power <- screen_models(set_power)
best_power <- pull_best(screened_power)
```

The screen is run again, and not assumed. Here
`r sum(check_sampling(set_power)$failed)` equation fails it, and not the one that
failed before, because a dispersion sub-model changes the posterior from which
each equation is sampled. The highest-weighted equation also receives more of the
weight in this set than in the constant-dispersion set (the messages from
`pull_best()` name each), and the weights themselves are more concentrated: under
constant dispersion `r sum(summary(screened_const)$mod_weights[, "wi"] > 0.01)`
equations share them, and under the sub-model
`r sum(summary(screened_power)$mod_weights[, "wi"] > 0.01)` do.

```{r disp-slope}
brms::fixef(pull_brmsfit(best_power))[c("c0_Intercept", "c1_Intercept"), ]
```

`c1` is the exponent (a value of zero corresponds to the constant-dispersion
model), so an interval excluding zero supports non-constant dispersion. The
coefficients of variation computed above provide an estimate of this exponent that
involves no fit, because a power law makes `log(cv)` linear in `log(mu)`, with
slope `-c1/2`.

```{r disp-slope-observed}
conc_mean <- tapply(cu$rlu_cens, cu$conc, mean)
obs_slope <- lm(log(cv_obs) ~ log(conc_mean))
# Multiplying by -2 reverses the order of the bounds, so the names confint()
# supplied would be attached to the wrong ends.
setNames(-2 * c(coef(obs_slope)[[2]], rev(confint(obs_slope)[2, ])),
         c("c1", "lower", "upper"))
c(r_squared = summary(obs_slope)$r.squared)
```

The fitted and observed estimates agree. The fit gives
`r sprintf("%.2f", brms::fixef(pull_brmsfit(best_power))["c1_Intercept", "Estimate"])`
(`r sprintf("%.2f", brms::fixef(pull_brmsfit(best_power))["c1_Intercept", "Q2.5"])`
to
`r sprintf("%.2f", brms::fixef(pull_brmsfit(best_power))["c1_Intercept", "Q97.5"])`),
and the wells give `r sprintf("%.2f", -2 * coef(obs_slope)[[2]])`
(`r sprintf("%.2f", -2 * confint(obs_slope)[2, 2])` to
`r sprintf("%.2f", -2 * confint(obs_slope)[2, 1])`). This agreement indicates that
the sub-model describes the replicate variation the design measured, and does not
absorb a failure of the curve elsewhere in the series. A within-concentration
standard deviation involves no curve, which is what makes that comparison worth
making. Whether a power law is the right form for the relationship is a separate
question, which is answered here by the regression, with an R-squared of
`r sprintf("%.3f", summary(obs_slope)$r.squared)` on `r summary(obs_slope)$df[2]`
degrees of freedom.

## Checking the dispersion sub-model

We repeat the within-concentration check after fitting `disp("power")`.

```{r checkfit-power}
cf_power <- check_fit(best_power)
c(flagged = sum(cf_power$ppp_sd > 0.95 | cf_power$ppp_sd < 0.05),
  groups = nrow(cf_power),
  control_sd_ratio = round(cf_power$sd_ratio[cf_power$control], 3))
```

For the highest-weighted equation, the number of groups flagged for scale falls
from `r sum(cf_const$ppp_sd > 0.95 | cf_const$ppp_sd < 0.05)` to
`r sum(cf_power$ppp_sd > 0.95 | cf_power$ppp_sd < 0.05)`, and the control ratio
changes from `r sprintf("%.2f", cf_const$sd_ratio[cf_const$control])` to
`r sprintf("%.2f", cf_power$sd_ratio[cf_power$control])`. Because the final
estimates use model averages, we also check every retained equation:

```{r checkfit-model-sets}
cf_const_set <- check_fit(screened_const)
cf_power_set <- check_fit(screened_power)
fit_scale_summary <- function(x, label) {
  parts <- split(as.data.frame(x), x$model)
  do.call(rbind, lapply(parts, function(d) {
    data.frame(dispersion = label, model = d$model[1], weight = d$wi[1],
               control_sd_ratio = d$sd_ratio[d$control],
               n_flagged_sd = sum(d$ppp_sd < 0.05 | d$ppp_sd > 0.95,
                                  na.rm = TRUE))
  }))
}
round_cols(rbind(fit_scale_summary(cf_const_set, "constant"),
                 fit_scale_summary(cf_power_set, "power")))
```

The model-set check gives the weight, control spread ratio and number of flagged
concentrations for each equation used in either average. It allows the local
comparison to be read beside each model's contribution, instead of treating the
highest-weighted equation as representative of the whole set.

`plot()` draws each statistic against the spread that the fit simulates for it,
and takes both fits at once where a `model` column distinguishes the panels.
Because the response falls from about
`r format(signif(max(cf_const$obs_mean), 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)`
relative light units at the control to about
`r format(signif(min(cf_const$obs_mean), 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)`
at the bottom, we put the display on a logarithmic scale, as on a linear scale the
lower half of the series would be a row of points on the axis.

```{r exmp9-disp-checkfit, fig.asp = 0.82, out.width = "92%"}
both <- rbind(cbind(cf_const, model = "constant"),
              cbind(cf_power, model = "disp(\"power\")"))
class(both) <- class(cf_const)
plot(both) +
  scale_y_log10(labels = function(x) {
    format(x, big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)
  })
```

Each bar is the 95% span of the statistic simulated from the fit, and each point
is the observed value. In the location panels the bars are wider under constant
dispersion, but all observed means fall inside their bars under both structures,
so the curve reproduces the response at all concentrations whichever dispersion
structure is used, and the mean was never what was wrong. The scale panels differ.
Under constant dispersion the observed standard deviation falls below its bar at
the `r sum(cf_const$ppp_sd > 0.95)` concentrations where the response is highest,
and the gap at the control is more than an order of magnitude
(`r format(signif(cf_const$obs_sd[cf_const$control], 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)`
observed, against a simulated span reaching
`r format(signif(cf_const$sim_sd_hi[cf_const$control], 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)`).
Under the sub-model the bars close on the points, and all observed standard
deviations fall inside their bars, including that of the control
(`r format(signif(cf_power$obs_sd[cf_power$control], 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)`
against a span of
`r format(signif(cf_power$sim_sd_lo[cf_power$control], 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)`
to
`r format(signif(cf_power$sim_sd_hi[cf_power$control], 3), big.mark = ",", scientific = FALSE)`).
A model can therefore describe the mean of each group and still be wrong about the
spread of all of them, which is the case singled out in the Overview, and here it
is the case that the sub-model corrects.

## The reported estimates

The estimates come from the screened set under the sub-model, shown here with its
credible band.

```{r exmp9-autoplot, fig.asp = 0.72, out.width = "80%"}
autoplot(screened_power) + copper_axis
```

The curve is the model-averaged prediction over the retained equations, and the
band is its 95% credible interval, both returned on the recorded concentration
scale, although the formula fitted `log(conc)`. The response remains above
`r round(100 * cf_const$obs_mean[4] / cf_const$obs_mean[1])` per cent of its
control value up to `r sprintf("%.2g", as.numeric(cf_const$group)[4])` mg/L, is at
`r round(100 * cf_const$obs_mean[6] / cf_const$obs_mean[1])` per cent of it by
`r sprintf("%.2g", as.numeric(cf_const$group)[6])`, and is below one per cent by
`r sprintf("%.2g", as.numeric(cf_const$group)[7])`, so the whole decline occurs
over a factor of about
`r round(as.numeric(cf_const$group)[7] / as.numeric(cf_const$group)[4])` in
concentration. The vertical lines mark the model-averaged *N(S)EC* and its bounds.

Compared with the constant-dispersion curve shown earlier (under *Fitting and
screening the set*), the band has closed onto the observations, and the *N(S)EC*
annotated on the figure has fallen with it. The curves under constant dispersion
and under the sub-model describe a common mean, and differ in how wide each makes
the posterior at the control, which is measured below.

The figure above illustrates the local discrepancy in the highest-weighted
equation; the table checks its prevalence across the retained sets. Estimates
are read from the screened sets because no single equation represents the model
average.

```{r disp-estimates}
rbind(constant = nsec(screened_const, xform = exp),
      power = nsec(screened_power, xform = exp))
rbind(constant = ecx(screened_const, ecx_val = 10, xform = exp),
      power = ecx(screened_power, ecx_val = 10, xform = exp))
```

The *NSEC* falls from `r sprintf("%.3f", nsec(screened_const, xform = exp)[1])` to
`r sprintf("%.3f", nsec(screened_power, xform = exp)[1])` mg/L, a factor of
`r sprintf("%.1f", nsec(screened_const, xform = exp)[1] / nsec(screened_power, xform = exp)[1])`.
The change arises through the quantity from which `nsec()` reads its reference,
which is the posterior of the control mean with any group-level deviation
excluded.

```{r control-posterior}
control_reference <- function(fit) {
  nd <- data.frame(conc = min(cu$conc), censoring = "none")
  post <- posterior_epred(pull_brmsfit(fit), newdata = nd, re_formula = NA)[, 1]
  c(sd = sd(post), cv = sd(post) / mean(post),
    reference_share = unname(quantile(post, 0.01) / mean(post)))
}
round(rbind(constant = control_reference(best_const),
            power = control_reference(best_power)), 4)
```

Under constant dispersion that posterior is
`r sprintf("%.0f", control_reference(best_const)[["sd"]] / control_reference(best_power)[["sd"]])`
times wider. Because `nsec()` sets its reference at a lower quantile of it, the
reference is at
`r sprintf("%.2f", control_reference(best_const)[["reference_share"]])` of the
control mean under constant dispersion, against
`r sprintf("%.2f", control_reference(best_power)[["reference_share"]])` under the
sub-model. A reference placed that far below the control is crossed further along
the curve, so the constant-dispersion fit returns the *less* protective figure,
and nothing in its sampler diagnostics indicates this.

The EC10 changes by less, from
`r sprintf("%.3f", ecx(screened_const, ecx_val = 10, xform = exp)[1])` to
`r sprintf("%.3f", ecx(screened_power, ecx_val = 10, xform = exp)[1])` mg/L,
whereas its interval narrows from
`r sprintf("%.3f", diff(ecx(screened_const, ecx_val = 10, xform = exp)[c(2, 3)]))`
to
`r sprintf("%.3f", diff(ecx(screened_power, ecx_val = 10, xform = exp)[c(2, 3)]))`
mg/L. Because the EC10 is measured at a fixed fraction of the control mean, and
not at a quantile of its posterior, the variance assumption affects it through the
precision of the curve, and not through the placement of its reference. This is
the difference between the *NSEC* and the ECx described in the Overview.

A methods section should state the dispersion structure under which each estimate
was fitted, because constant dispersion and the sub-model return different
estimates from the same data.

# Summary

The sampler screen returned different outcomes for both prior choices on
the 2050 colour data. At the default sampling length, it removed
`r sum(sampling_default$failed)` of `r nrow(sampling_default)` fitted equations
under uninformative priors and `r sum(sampling_regular$failed)` of
`r nrow(sampling_regular)` under regularising priors. On `nassarius`, also at
the defaults, it removed `r sum(check_sampling(fit_nass)$failed)` of
`r nrow(check_sampling(fit_nass))`. The equations and reasons should be
reported for each fit; a prior choice that reduces failures in one model may worsen
another.

The diagnostic table separates inadequate effective sample size from divergent
transitions and failed initialisation. A longer run may resolve an ESS-only
failure, whereas divergences call for examining posterior geometry, priors and
parameterisation. Sparse concentration designs may contribute to these
difficulties, particularly for equations with more curve parameters, but the
diagnostics alone do not establish that explanation. Excluding an equation at
the stated cutoff is an analysis decision that should be reported.

The variance assumption is the one that no sampler diagnostic detects. On the
`lum31` copper plate the observed coefficient of variation ran from
`r sprintf("%.3f", cv_obs[1])` at the control to
`r sprintf("%.3f", cv_obs[length(cv_obs)])` at the bottom of the series, and the
constant-dispersion fit passed the sampler screen while its highest-weighted
equation simulated about
`r round(1 / cf_const$sd_ratio[cf_const$control])` times the spread observed at
the control. Adding `disp("power")` removed the groupwise flags for that equation
and reduced the model-averaged *NSEC* from
`r sprintf("%.3f", nsec(screened_const, xform = exp)[1])` to
`r sprintf("%.3f", nsec(screened_power, xform = exp)[1])` mg/L. A fit can
therefore pass all the checks the sampler screen applies and still simulate the
wrong spread at the concentration from which both estimators are referenced.

This distinction also applies to the decisions made around the fit.
`binomial` was retained after examining dispersion across the screened
equations; the family for a continuous bounded
response was chosen on the support of the data and not on a test; and the scale on
which each estimate is reported depends on `xform`. None of these is a result that
the workflow produces automatically, and each is a decision that the analysis
needs to record.

